-------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Introduction
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
This file includes instructions for building SAC and its accompanying
libraries and utilities from sources.  After installing, see README.md in
this directory for setting environment variables and related setup.

These instructions apply to both a git checkout and an unpacked release
tarball (sac-VERSION.tar.gz).  The top directory of the tree is the
directory that contains configure.  File src/describedirs has a brief
overview of the contents of the src/ subdirectories.

A committed configure script is shipped with the sources; for a normal
build you do not need to run ./bootstrap.  Rebuild configure with
./bootstrap only when changing configure.ac or related autotools input.

The build goes best with GNU gcc and GNU make
(https://www.gnu.org/software/).

Building SAC requires development packages for:
  - X11      (graphics; on macOS install XQuartz, https://www.xquartz.org/)
  - libcurl  (internet requests)
  - libxml2  (XML)

XPM support is enabled automatically when libXpm is found.  Starting with
v101.6, PNG support is off by default because (1) the resolution is far
worse than PDF and PS, and (2) PNG libraries are typically dynamic, so a
binary built on one computer may not be portable.  Include PNG with
--enable-png.

Command line editing and history are provided by a vendored copy of
linenoise, so no external editing library is required.  Use
--disable-editing to build without command line editing.

* Quick Instructions for Building and Installing SAC:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
From the source tree (the directory that contains configure), use an
out-of-tree build:

% mkdir build
% cd build
% ../configure --prefix=/usr/local/sac > ../build.out
% make >> ../build.out
% make install >> ../build.out

--prefix=/usr/local/sac is the default; pass a different value to install
elsewhere (for example --prefix=/opt/sac).  The source tree is left
unchanged.  After install, SACHOME is the same as the prefix used here.

Redirecting stdout to ../build.out is optional; it reduces noise from
configure and make while leaving warnings and errors on the terminal
(stderr).

* Detailed Instructions for Building and Installing SAC:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
From the source tree (or from build/ with ../configure):

% ./configure --help
  Lists configure options.  Options of interest:

    --prefix=PREFIX         install location [default: /usr/local/sac]
    --enable-optim=level    compile with -Olevel (for example --enable-optim=2);
                            default is no optimization; floating-point contraction
                            is disabled when the compiler supports it, so results
                            match the test suite baselines
    --enable-debug          enable debugging options (mainly for developers)
    --disable-editing       build without command line editing and history
    --with-matlab=DIR       enable Matlab support using Matlab installed in DIR
    --enable-xdr            enable XDR file format for data files
    --enable-zlib           enable compressed PDF files
    --enable-xpm            try to enable XPM plot output (default: on if found)
    --enable-png            enable PNG plot output (default: off)
    --x-includes=DIR        X11 headers in DIR
    --x-libraries=DIR       X11 libraries in DIR
    --with-libcurl=PREFIX   look for libcurl under PREFIX

configure writes Makefiles.  Then:

% make                        - build sac and libraries (under src/ in the
                                build tree, along with other lib*.a files)
% make install                - install into ${PREFIX}
% make clean                  - remove objects and executables, keep config
% make distclean              - like clean, also remove configuration files

On Solaris, use gmake instead of the system make.  make clean and
make distclean print every deletion; redirect to /dev/null if desired.

* Testing
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
After a successful build, from the build directory:

% make check

On a headless Linux system, run the tests under a virtual X server, for
example:

% xvfb-run -a make check

* Platforms
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
Current CI builds and tests Linux and macOS.  Successful builds have also
been reported historically on Solaris and Windows (Cygwin), given the
required development tools.

Cygwin: XDR support needs the Sun RPC library from the Cygwin setup
program.  As of Cygwin 1.7 the normal source tree builds; a separate
CYGWIN distribution is not provided.

macOS: Install XQuartz.  If configure does not find X11, pass for example:

   % ../configure --x-includes=/opt/X11/include --x-libraries=/opt/X11/lib

Linux: Install the X11, libcurl, and libxml2 development packages for your
distribution.  On a dual 32/64-bit system, ensure configure links the
64-bit X11 libraries when building a 64-bit sac; override with
--x-libraries if needed.

* Creating the documentation
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
Help text lives under aux/help/ as reStructuredText.  From doc/, it can be
converted to a set of HTML pages and/or a single PDF manual.  This is a
manual developer/release step; it is not run by configure, make, or
make install.

Building either form requires Python 3 with docutils installed
(https://docutils.sourceforge.io/): rst2html or rst2html.py on PATH for
HTML, and an importable docutils package for PDF.  Building the PDF also
requires pdflatex with a normal TeX Live/MacTeX package set.

To build the HTML pages:

% cd doc
% ./help2html_new.sh all

This writes pages under doc/html/ (and doc/html/commands/, doc/html/manual/,
and so on), mirroring the aux/help/ layout.  To rebuild a single page
instead of everything, pass its path relative to doc/, for example
../aux/help/fft.

To build the PDF manual:

% cd doc
% ./help2html_new.sh -pdf all
% ./rst2sac_new.py
% cd html
% pdflatex sac_manual.tex
% pdflatex sac_manual.tex

-pdf must come before all.  The first command stages the help text as
.txt files under html/; rst2sac_new.py converts those to per-page .tex
files; pdflatex then builds html/sac_manual.pdf from the master document
html/sac_manual.tex (itself copied from html/sac_manual.tex.in).  Run
pdflatex twice so the table of contents is populated.

To discard generated HTML/TeX/PDF output and restore the master TeX file
and title-page PDF from their .in originals:

% cd doc
% ./clean_html.sh

Run the HTML and/or PDF steps again afterward to regenerate them.  The
manual's version string is kept in sync with configure.ac by
config/set-version; see Cutting a release below.

* Cutting a release
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
The version is authored in one place: the AC_INIT line in configure.ac.
Everything built through configure (the version banner, sac-config,
distribution tarball names, the macOS .dmg) is derived from it
automatically.  A few files that configure does not reach are kept in
sync by config/set-version.

1. Edit the version in configure.ac (AC_INIT, second argument).
2. Run ./bootstrap and commit the regenerated configure along with
   configure.ac.  CI builds from the committed configure rather than
   running bootstrap, so an uncommitted regeneration ships the previous
   version.
3. Run config/set-version and commit the files it updates.
4. Add a YYYY/MM Version X.Y released heading to CHANGES.
5. Confirm everything agrees with config/set-version --check.
6. Tag vX.Y, matching the two-component configure.ac version, and push
   the tag.  The release workflow publishes a draft, prerelease GitHub
   release, which still needs manual promotion.
